- How would the solution change if cooldown length varied to c days?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with a non-adjacent removal constraint and requires reconstruction of which indices were removed.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP state dp[i][t][r]
- Non-adjacent constraint during removal
- Path reconstruction for the chosen indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxRemainingSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY / 4;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: k + 1 }, () => [NEG, NEG])
  );
  dp[0][0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // keep i
      dp[i][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t][0], dp[i-1][t][1]) + A[i-1];
      // remove i
      if (t > 0) dp[i][t][1] = dp[i-1][t-1][0];
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n][k][0], dp[n][k][1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to reduce space to O(k)?
- How to modify for at-most-k removals?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests a foundational dynamic programming pattern—selecting k non-adjacent elements with maximum sum. It assesses understanding of state definition, transition logic, and reconstruction, making it a classic beginner DP problem.

## Key Concepts
- **State definition**: dp[i][j] represents the optimal sum for the first i elements with exactly j selections
- **Transition logic**: Compare skipping the current element vs. taking it (with the appropriate previous state)
- **Backtracking**: Reconstruct chosen indices by comparing states during backtracking
- **Feasibility constraint**: Maximum selections possible in i elements is floor((i+1)/2)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumNonAdjacent(A, m) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(m + 1).fill(NEG));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= Math.min(m, Math.floor((i + 1) / 2)); j++) {
      const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
      const skip = dp[i - 1][j];
      dp[i][j] = Math.max(skip, take);
    }
  }

  const result = dp[n][m];
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, j = m;

  while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
    const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
    if (dp[i][j] === take) {
      indices.push(i - 1);
      j--;
      i -= 2;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { sum: result, indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(n × m)
**Space Complexity:** O(n × m), or O(m) with optimization.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a fresh DP angle: interval-based value depends on distinct characters within each substring, plus precise reconstruction for k blocks.

## Key Concepts

- Non-overlapping interval DP
- Precomputing distinct counts for all substrings
- Reconstruction via back-pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
function maxKSubstrings(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const freq = new Array(26).fill(0);
    let setBits = 0;
    for (let j = i; j >= 0; j--) {
      const idx = s.charCodeAt(j) - 97;
      if (freq[idx]++ === 0) setBits++;
      dist[j][i] = setBits;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  const start = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][0] = 0;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Option 1: skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        prev[i][t] = i-1;
        start[i][t] = start[i-1][t];
      }

      // Option 2: end t-th substring at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const val = dp[s-1][t-1] + dist[s-1][i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = s-1;
          start[i][t] = s-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct substrings
  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t] === i-1) {
      i--;
    } else {
      result.unshift([start[i][t]+1, i]);
      i = prev[i][t];
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], substrings: result };
}
```

Time complexity: O(n²k) for DP transitions + O(n²) for preprocessing. Space: O(n² + nk). Optimizations include limiting the inner loop or using convex hull tricks for special cases.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests building a DP over subsequences with constraints, requiring a clear state definition and transition over previous indices, plus reconstruction capabilities.

## Key Concepts
- Subsequence DP with constraints
- State dp[i] and parent tracking
- Time/space analysis and reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSubseqWithDiff(a, d) {
  const n = a.length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(0);
  const par = Array(n).fill(-1);
  let best = -Infinity, bestIdx = -1;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    dp[i] = a[i];
    for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
      if (Math.abs(a[i] - a[j]) <= d && dp[j] + a[i] > dp[i]) {
        dp[i] = dp[j] + a[i];
        par[i] = j;
      }
    }
    if (dp[i] > best) {
      best = dp[i];
      bestIdx = i;
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct subsequence
  const seq = [];
  let curr = bestIdx;
  while (curr !== -1) {
    seq.push(curr);
    curr = par[curr];
  }
  return { maxSum: best, subsequence: seq.reverse() };
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity
Time: O(n²) due to nested loops checking all previous indices
Space: O(n) for dp and parent arrays

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model partitioning with a simple per-block objective and reconstruct optimal cuts. It reveals DP formulation, range queries, and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP across two dimensions (prefix and blocks)
- Range maximum precomputation or on-the-fly tracking
- Backpointers for path reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPartitionCostMax(A, m){
  const n = A.length;
  // precompute max for all ranges
  const rangeMax = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    let cur = 0;
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      cur = Math.max(cur, A[j]);
      rangeMax[i][j] = cur;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(m+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let j=1;j<=Math.min(i,m);j++){
      for(let t=j-1; t<=i-1; t++){
        const val = dp[t][j-1] + rangeMax[t][i-1];
        if(val < dp[i][j]){
          dp[i][j] = val;
          par[i][j] = t;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  const cuts = [];
  let i = n, j = m;
  while(j>0){
    const t = par[i][j];
    cuts.push(i);
    i = t;
    j--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {cost: dp[n][m], partitions: cuts};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(n m) time using monotonic queues?  
- How would you adapt if each chapter cost were max(A[t..i-1]) + c*(i-t)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests palindrome enumeration and multi-substring DP; requires handling non-overlapping constraints and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Palindrome precomputation isPal[l][r] in O(n^2).
- DP states dp[i][t] track best total length for first i chars and t palindromes.
- Transition closes a palindrome ending at i via isPal[l][i-1], adding (i-l+1).
- Backpointers enable reconstruction of the endpoints.
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(n^2 + nk).

## Code Example
```javascript
// Precompute isPal
for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
  for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
    isPal[i][j] = s[i] === s[j] && (j - i < 2 || isPal[i + 1][j - 1]);
  }
}

// DP and reconstruction
const NEG = -Infinity;
let dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(NEG));
let prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
dp[0][0] = 0;
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
    if (t > 0) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (isPal[l - 1][i - 1] && dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== NEG) {
          const cand = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (i - l + 1);
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            prev[i][t] = [l - 1, t - 1, i - 1];
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP partitioning with a non-sum objective and reconstruction requirements.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions with dp[i][t]
- Precomputing max-min for all subarrays
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxMinSubarrayVal(arr){
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxVal = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  const minVal = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    let curMax = arr[i], curMin = arr[i];
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      curMax = Math.max(curMax, arr[j]);
      curMin = Math.min(curMin, arr[j]);
      maxVal[i][j] = curMax;
      minVal[i][j] = curMin;
    }
  }
  const val = (l,r)=> maxVal[l][r] - minVal[l][r];
  return val;
}

function bestPartition(arr, k){
  const n = arr.length;
  const val = maxMinSubarrayVal(arr);
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = val(0,i-1);
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] + val(j, i-1);
        if(cand > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = cand; parent[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let blocks = [];
  let i=n, t=k;
  while(t>0){
    const j = parent[i][t];
    blocks.push([j, i-1]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to optimize memory to O(nk)?
- Can you handle ties deterministically for block reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests comprehensive DP design for partitioning problems with nonlinear segment costs involving both min and sum operations, requiring careful handling of base cases and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-dimensional DP over partition indices
- Range minimum query optimization within transitions
- Complete solution from base cases through reconstruction
- Space-time tradeoffs and optimization strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Initialize DP table and parent pointers
dp[0][0] = 0;
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  dp[i][1] = rangeMin(1, i) * prefix[i];
  parent[i][1] = 0;
}

// Fill DP table
for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
  for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let p = t-1; p < i; p++) {
      const segVal = rangeMin(p+1, i) * (prefix[i] - prefix[p]);
      const total = dp[p][t-1] + segVal;
      if (total > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = total;
        parent[i][t] = p;
      }
    }
  }
}

// Reconstruct segments
let i = n, t = k;
while (t > 0) {
  const p = parent[i][t];
  segments[t] = [p+1, i];
  i = p;
  t--;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would handle negative numbers and at-most-k segments?
- Can you optimize to O(nk log n) using monotone queues?
- What modifications needed for weighted segment costs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with partitioning and a nontrivial per-substring cost, plus backtracking to recover partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t]
- Precompute cost[l][r] in O(n^2) via two-pointer mismatches
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPalindromePartitions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      let a = l, b = r, c = 0;
      while (a < b) { if (s[a] !== s[b]) c++; a++; b--; }
      cost[l][r] = c;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + cost[p + 1][i];
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; parent[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  const splits = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t >= 1) {
    splits.push(i);
    i = parent[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  splits.reverse();
  return { minCost: dp[n - 1][k], partitions: splits };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize space for large n or small k?
- How would this adapt if cost used a different per-substring metric?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests substring DP and reconstruction in a practical string-cleanup task, common in data processing pipelines at scale.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings: dp[i][j] captures minimal deletions for s[i..j]
- Reconstruction: recover exact deletions and resulting palindrome
- Complexity: O(n^2) time and O(n^2) space; can be optimized with path tracking

## Code Example
```javascript
function minDeletionsPalindrome(s) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      if (s[i] === s[j]) dp[i][j] = dp[i+1][j-1];
      else dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1]) + 1;
    }
  }
  return {deletions: dp[0][n-1], dp};
}
```

